WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.917
This query shows one of the safest patterns for analytical joins.

00:00:03.917 --> 00:00:10.357
Instead of joining every detail row first, we summarize each child table before the join.

00:00:10.357 --> 00:00:14.617
The admission-count CTE produces one row per patient.

00:00:14.617 --> 00:00:18.797
The lab-count CTE also produces one row per patient.

00:00:18.797 --> 00:00:21.957
The prescription-count CTE does the same.

00:00:21.957 --> 00:00:27.077
Only after those sources are at the same grain do we join them to the patient table.

00:00:27.077 --> 00:00:31.767
Now the final result is easy to defend: one row per patient.

00:00:31.767 --> 00:00:39.127
Patients with no activity remain because we use LEFT JOIN, and COALESCE converts missing counts to zero.

00:00:39.127 --> 00:00:42.387
This pattern is very common in dashboards.

00:00:42.387 --> 00:00:49.062
Suppose a customer dashboard needs total orders, total support tickets, and total returns.

00:00:49.062 --> 00:00:53.932
A safe design is to summarize each activity table by customer first,

00:00:53.932 --> 00:00:57.157
then join the summaries to the customer master.

00:00:57.157 --> 00:01:03.227
The SQL is a little longer, but the result is much easier to validate and explain.

00:01:03.227 --> 00:01:08.442
In analytics, a slightly longer query that clearly protects the target grain is usually better than a shorter

00:01:08.442 --> 00:01:12.240
query that produces ambiguous counts.
